# Continuous integration

Pull requests and pushes run both mock suites on Ubuntu 22.04 and 24.04,
Bash/POSIX-shell syntax and error-level ShellCheck, Perl compile checks,
actionlint, staged installation tests, RPM build/install tests on Rocky Linux 9,
man/HTML generation, version consistency, and source archive checks. CodeQL
analyzes GitHub Actions workflows, not the Bash or Perl application code.

Run the fast checks with:

    make -C t functional
    make -C t functional-gpu
    bash .ci/check-source.sh
    bash .ci/install-smoke.sh

Source checks require ShellCheck and Perl Switch. Broader ShellCheck warnings
are not yet enforced. SC2145 is baselined only for four existing logging calls
in src/sbank-common; all other error-level rules apply. Vendored shFlags is
exercised by the functional tests rather than held to the application's lint
baseline. Docs/archive checks additionally require groff; HTML needs ikiwiki.
The scripts assume a repository checkout, except the installed commands under test.

The separate Real Slurm accounting integration workflow runs manually or
weekly on the default branch. It builds an Ubuntu 26.04 image and starts its
own MariaDB, Munge, SlurmDBD, controller, and compute daemon. No external
cluster configuration, mounts, credentials, or Slurm services are used.
To reproduce it on a machine with Docker:

    docker build -f .ci/slurm/Dockerfile -t sbank-slurm-ci .
    mkdir -p artifacts
    docker run --rm --hostname sbank-ci -v "$PWD/artifacts:/artifacts" sbank-slurm-ci

The fixture creates/deletes an account, verifies CPU and GPU credit/debit and
read-back, completes an in-budget GPU job, requires an over-budget job to stay
pending for a GPU-minute accounting reason, then deposits credit and requires
that same job to finish. It also holds the production advisory lock while
another real CLI process tries to deposit; that process must fail without
changing the account. Daemon and accounting logs are uploaded on failure.

Its /dev/null GPU device is a stand-in for scheduling and GPU-minute accounting.
It does not validate GPU hardware, drivers, device isolation, or cross-host
filesystem locking. Cgroups are disabled in this fixture. The real integration
workflow needs its first successful hosted run before being promoted to a
required PR check; the scripts fail if readiness or assertions time out.

The RPM job installs the built package with dependency resolution and runs both
mock suites against installed files. The spec declares the Switch, flock and
other command dependencies used by the application. Existing live `make test`
is deliberately not invoked against a shared or production Slurm cluster.

Actions use commit pins, read-only checkout credentials, explicit permissions,
timeouts, concurrency cancellation, and retained functional/integration logs.
Dependabot retains its existing GitHub Actions update policy and also watches
the Dockerfile base image. Distribution package versions are not locked; these
jobs test current repositories for the selected distributions. No release or
HTML documentation is automatically published. Update required-check names in
branch protection after replacing the two previous functional workflows.
